<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu Ding</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu Ding was a king of the Shang dynasty who reigned for several decades in the thirteenth century BCE from the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinxu">Yinxu</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a>. The earliest known oracle-bone inscriptions date from his reign and record wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tufang">Tufang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guifang">Guifang</a> and Qiang, royal hunts and rituals. His consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-hao">Fu Hao</a>, whose intact tomb was found in 1976, led armies on his behalf.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-ding.jpg" alt="Wu Ding" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wang Qiji of the Ming Dynasty, Wanli engraved &quot;Three Talents Picture Club · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%AD%A6%E4%B8%81.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wu Ding, whose personal name was Zi Zhao, is the earliest figure in Chinese history mentioned in contemporary records. Later historians had thought the annals of the Shang dynasty little more than legend until oracle bone inscriptions from his reign were unearthed at his capital Yin, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a>, in 1899. They have been radiocarbon dated to 1254 to 1197 BCE, give or take ten years. The Shang practised succession by agnatic seniority, and before him succession had been split between two lines. His predecessors were three of his uncles, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-geng">Pan Geng</a>, who moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huanbei-shang-city">Huanbei</a>, and then his father Xiao Yi. A tradition says he was sent away from the capital in his father's reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Shiji lists him as the twenty-second Shang king, while the inscriptions record him as the twenty-first. Chapter XXI of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a> describes him staying silent at the start of his reign, even after three years of mourning, in order to cultivate his virtue. He first appointed his former tutor Gan Pan chief minister. After Gan Pan died, the chancellor was Fu Yue, whom he found, according to the Shiji and the Shangshu, after Heaven told him in a dream to expect such a man. His realm grew far larger than his ancestors', but control of distant lands was nominal, and he delegated power to regional lords who paid tribute, harvest portions and military support.</p><p>Oracle bones record wars with the fang peoples. According to the Bamboo Annals, in his thirty-second year he sent troops against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guifang">Guifang</a> and after three years annexed them. A Shang force of up to thirteen thousand fought Ba under the command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-hao">Fu Hao</a>, but Ba stayed independent. Women held greater official power than in later periods: his consort Fu Hao was a military commander, and Fu Jing seems to have overseen agriculture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in the fifty-ninth year of his reign according to all sources, none of them contemporary. His year of death is unknown, with opinions ranging from 1197 to 1180 BCE and 1192 BCE commonly accepted. Tomb 1400 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xibeigang">Xibeigang</a> is very likely his grave. Later tradition regarded him as one of the most esteemed Shang kings. Subsequent kings honoured him on the ding day, and he received the temple name Gaozong. His descendants addressed him as Grandfather Ding and Ancestor Ding. More than half of all Shang inscriptions date from his reign, and the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oracle-bone-script">oracle bone script</a> on tortoise plastrons and ox scapulae, with divination about war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> and the economy, was unearthed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinxu">Yinxu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wu Ding” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…credited with moving the capital to Yin, near modern Anyang in Henan, around 1300 BCE, and the inscriptions found there, which begin in the reign of <b>Wu Ding</b> in the thirteenth century BCE, make the late Shang the first period of Chinese history documented by its own writing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
